504
00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:50.000
Right away, Andy's going to tell Daryl that he's talking to Rachel.

505
00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:55.320
You know, he met her at Daryl's party, and he's going to be taking her out to dinner at

506
00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.060
Blue Wasabi.

507
00:22:56.600 --> 00:23:00.200
Yeah, Phyllis says they'll make a burger for you if you cause a scene.

508
00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:01.940
Yeah, she kind of throws that away.

509
00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:03.180
It's kind of like tucked under.

510
00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:05.660
Well, we got a fan question from Daria R.

511
00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:07.680
in Ontario, Canada, who said,

512
00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:11.500
Blue Wasabi is a sushi restaurant in Clark Summit, Pennsylvania.

513
00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:16.480
It is now permanently closed, but it looks like they don't make burgers.

514
00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:20.040
So it's funny that Phyllis mentioned that you have to make a scene to get one.

515
00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:25.900
I'll have you know, there is a Blue Wasabi in Gilbert, Arizona that does have a Buddha

516
00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:27.200
burger on their menu.

517
00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:32.180
So you can get a burger at that Blue Wasabi, but not at the one in Pennsylvania that is

518
00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:32.680
now closed.

519
00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.920
Well, maybe Phyllis went there and made a big old scene, and now it's on the menu.

520
00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:36.520
Perhaps.

521
00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.480
Phyllis has been to Arizona.

522
00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:44.520
We're going to start the whole reason this episode was titled PDA with Michael and Holly.

523
00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:48.040
They're kissing each other goodbye before they go back to their respective deaths.

524
00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:50.060
They can't get enough of each other.

525
00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:54.820
They even do this little shimmy kiss hug all the way back to Michael's office.

526
00:23:54.820 --> 00:23:58.460
I want you to know, that was improvised, the shimmy kiss.

527
00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:00.880
That was just Steve and Amy having some fun there.

528
00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:06.520
That backward scoot was my personal favorite, and I think if you look at 1 minute 52 seconds,

529
00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:09.160
you can kind of see Ed Helms starting to break.

530
00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:09.860
I'm sure.

531
00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:12.160
Because we weren't expecting it.

532
00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:12.660
Uh-huh.

533
00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:13.600
And it was amazing.

534
00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:15.320
They would do different things each time.

535
00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.340
I caught us breaking throughout this whole episode.

536
00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:19.520
I believe it.

537
00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:27.480
Next up, Erin is going to get a beautiful bouquet of flowers from Gabe, and when she opens the

538
00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:34.740
card, she realizes that there is going to be a romantic treasure hunt, not to be confused

539
00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:35.780
with a scavenger hunt.

540
00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:36.800
This is a treasure hunt.

541
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:37.140
Yeah.

542
00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:40.940
Gabe has a talking head that says, I dominate Valentine's Day.

543
00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:43.560
I practically make romance into a science.

544
00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:48.860
On the commentary, Greg shared that his note to Zach for this talking head was,

545
00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:54.600
imagine he was like a UFC fighter, like a mixed martial artist, like kind of calling out to

546
00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:55.360
his other opponent.

547
00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:57.440
I dominate Valentine's Day.

548
00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:02.340
Erin's first clue is a puzzle.

549
00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:07.840
She's going to have to put this puzzle together to figure out the next step in the treasure

550
00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:08.220
hunt.

551
00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:11.020
We got a fan question from Laura W.

552
00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:15.700
in La Crosse, Washington, related to the puzzle that Erin

553
00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.140
and Andy are putting together.

554
00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:23.840
Laura says, when I was in college, I worked a lot of random odd jobs, and one of them was for a tiny

555
00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:27.960
custom puzzle-making facility right here in La Crosse, Washington.

556
00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:30.620
It's a family-owned and run business.

557
00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:32.060
They are the sweetest people.

558
00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:39.500
At one point, we were quizzing the owner on his weirdest custom puzzles, and he said they've gotten

559
00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:45.560
to make a few famous puzzles, including the one for this episode of The Office.

560
00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:46.200
Hey!

561
00:25:46.820 --> 00:25:53.120
Laura says, Wisconsin doesn't get a lot of movie magic opportunities, so shout out to Portrait Puzzles.

562
00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:54.560
Yes!

563
00:25:54.980 --> 00:26:02.280
Our set designer, Michael Gallenberg, took three photos, and Phil Shea used Portrait Puzzles to

564
00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:03.580
make three different puzzles.

565
00:26:03.580 --> 00:26:06.340
Then Greg got to choose the one he liked beSt.

566
00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:10.160
The company Portrait Puzzles have been in business since 2005.

567
00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:17.400
They still make custom puzzles today, anywhere from 16-piece postcard puzzles to 30-piece puzzles,

568
00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:18.560
1,000-piece puzzles.

569
00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:19.780
They ship to the U.S.

570
00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:24.000
and Canada, and they range in price from $29.99 to $49.99.

571
00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:24.780
Aw!

572
00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:27.320
We need an office ladies puzzle!

573
00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:29.760
I like this idea!

574
00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.060
Portrait Puzzles, we might be calling you!

575
00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:37.540
Well, Jenna, I looked into my digital clutter for the week that we filmed this episode.

576
00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:38.000
Okay.

577
00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:40.720
We were emailing each other.

578
00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:41.840
About what?

579
00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:43.000
You want to hear?

580
00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:43.420
Yeah!

581
00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.220
This tickled me so much.

582
00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:47.700
All right, here's our email exchange.

583
00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:53.480
First, I emailed you, and I said, hey, I'm going to try a new recipe for dinner tonight.

584
00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:55.340
Here's what I'm making.

585
00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.100
Spicy Shrimp Coconut Rice.

586
00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:00.600
And then I sent you the recipe.

587
00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:01.400
Oh!

588
00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.600
You wrote back, how spicy are you making it?

589
00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.900
I wrote back and said, pretty spicy.

590
00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:11.360
I know, we're such jerks.

591
00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:17.120
Then I wrote you, and I said, hey, I'm on set, and Patrice has your Redbook.

592
00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.240
So Patrice was our set medic.

593
00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:21.720
She sat right by the back door as you walked in.

594
00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:28.240
Jenna, you were on the cover of Redbook magazine, and Patrice had bought it, and I wrote you,

595
00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:30.180
and I said, I just read your interview.

596
00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:31.420
It is great!

597
00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:33.080
Three exclamation points.

598
00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.820
I felt like it was such an awesome interview, and you look fantastic.

599
00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:38.560
Oh my gosh!

600
00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.940
So then I went and looked for the cover of the Redbook magazine.

601
00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:43.740
Jenna, you look amazing.

602
00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:44.900
I'm going to put it in stories.

603
00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:48.860
The article read, Jenna Fisher, totally candid and too cute.

604
00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:54.660
And this was the opening paragraph of the interview written by the writer, Laurie Sandell.

605
00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.960
I have to gush on my BFF because I thought it was so adorable.

606
00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:00.900
This was what Laurie wrote about meeting you.

607
00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:06.700
Jenna Fisher's quote, How I Met My Husband story, sounds like the plot of a quirky indie romance,

608
00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:09.860
which is fitting since they fell in love while working on one.

609
00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:13.380
I'm hearing all the details over dinner at the Beverly Hills Hotel.

610
00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:17.020
Rigatoni with short ribs for Jenna, tuna tartare for me.

611
00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:22.160
She arrived five minutes early for the interview, unheard of in the celebrity world.

612
00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:27.880
Walking right over to me with a wide smile, like she might be about to ask my advice on a gym dilemma.

613
00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:31.780
In dress pants, a gray cardigan, and a pretty red blouse,

614
00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:35.420
Jenna looks as all-American as two scoops of vanilla ice cream.

615
00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:40.680
And as we start to dish about love, fate, and favorite nail polish colors, parentheses,

616
00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.320
she's loving Essie's Angora Cardi.

617
00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:47.500
I have to keep reminding myself that I don't actually know her.

618
00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.100
She's so laid back.

619
00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:53.560
She could easily pass for your fun friend from high school, just like on The Office.

620
00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:54.960
My gosh!

621
00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:56.080
I know!

622
00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:58.300
I made a good impression that day.

623
00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:01.620
Well, reading that made me smile because that's exactly who you are, Jenna.

624
00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:06.600
You do feel like everyone's best friend because you're just such a nice, kind person.

625
00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:09.780
Why are you making me cry today?

626
00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:10.520
What is happening?

627
00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:12.340
What is going on?

628
00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.440
Am I dying?

629
00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:15.160
No!

630
00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:16.460
Is this my memorial?

631
00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:17.100
Stop it!

632
00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:17.980
This is so kind!

633
00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:18.900
Stop it!

634
00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:20.000
Oh my gosh!

635
00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:23.680
I just was really happy that Lori wrote that because that's how I feel.

636
00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.100
I was not expecting that.

637
00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:33.340
I was just ready to talk about the scene of Oscar watching Michael and Holly do some hand sex.

638
00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.120
Some heavy hand petting?

639
00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:36.620
Yes!

640
00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:37.880
Thank you!

641
00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:39.280
Thanks for digging that out.

642
00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:42.400
I actually completely remember doing that interview.

643
00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:43.420
I loved her.

644
00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:44.420
She was so nice.

645
00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:46.340
She felt like an instant friend, too.

646
00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:50.540
So, you know, she must have come into the interview with a warmth and it was disarming.

647
00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:51.920
And we had a lovely dinner.

648
00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:56.280
Well, I'm going to put the photo and stories of you on the cover and the article.

649
00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:59.320
And I'm also going to put my spicy shrimp coconut recipe.

650
00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:00.240
Please do.

651
00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:02.660
I love my question was how spicy.

652
00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:03.340
Mm-hmm.

653
00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:04.660
Real spicy.

654
00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:06.280
That was before texting.

655
00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:08.200
Now we would just text each other that.

656
00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:08.580
I know.

657
00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.660
You guys, this is when we had flip phones.

658
00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:11.680
I love it.

659
00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:17.300
Well, now a very giggly Pam and Jim are going to arrive to the office via a cab.

660
00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:19.720
They are completely tooted.

661
00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.160
It's true.

662
00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:26.720
We had a fan question from Rochelle R.

663
00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:29.880
in El Dorado, California, who said,

664
00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:34.660
Please help settle a decades-long friendly debate between my husband and I.

665
00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:40.800
My husband insists that you all are just incredibly talented actors, but come on.

666
00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.600
You and John were really drunk, right?

667
00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:44.820
Don't worry.

668
00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:46.540
Your secret is safe with me.

669
00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:49.940
Rochelle, we were totally sober.

670
00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:51.460
They were totally sober.

671
00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.500
They just flippin' crushed it.

672
00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:55.920
I have to brag on you one second here.

673
00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:56.960
What is happening?

674
00:30:57.140 --> 00:30:58.080
Well, listen now.

675
00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:03.120
I said this on the commentary, and as I rewatched and heard myself from years ago, I agreed with

676
00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:07.780
myself, which is, it's one thing to have to play drunk in one scene.

677
00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:09.280
That's hard enough.

678
00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:11.580
You had to play drunk all week.

679
00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:12.600
All week.

680
00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.640
And you had to match the same tone.

681
00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:21.820
We also had a long conversation about how it feels to day drink and be a little tooted,

682
00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:22.800
as you would say.

683
00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:26.440
And then you're slowly sobering up.

684
00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:31.780
And you know the kind of like, it doesn't feel great about three hours after you've done

685
00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:32.400
day drinking.

686
00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:33.580
You're kind of tired.

687
00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:34.980
It kind of ends in a nap.

688
00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:42.040
But that moment by the cab where Pam drops her scarf, I guess I'm going to pat myself on

689
00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:42.600
the back now.

690
00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:44.820
That was my idea on the day.

691
00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:49.900
So originally in the script, it was scripted that Jim was going to have a hard time counting

692
00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:52.160
the cash to the cab driver.

693
00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:55.840
That was how we were going to reveal that we were kind of drunk and we were giggling because

694
00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:57.240
Jim couldn't count his money.

695
00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:03.520
But Greg wanted to move the camera into an extreme spy shot where they're really catching

696
00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:05.340
us through the upstairs window.

697
00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:07.100
Oh, so you wouldn't see the counting.

698
00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:08.020
It wasn't reading.

699
00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:12.220
So we had to come up with something else because Greg really didn't want us to see the camera.

700
00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:14.140
He didn't want the camera down in the parking lot.

701
00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:20.600
So I am always dropping stuff like that when I have had a couple of drinks.

702
00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:26.380
So like in New York City, if Lee and I go out to dinner and I have a couple of glasses

703
00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:33.160
of wine, you can count on me leaving a glove or my scarf or something on the side of the

704
00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:34.200
road or in a taxi.

705
00:32:34.760 --> 00:32:41.780
So I pitched this idea and I managed to drop the scarf and that whole bit, we kind of found

706
00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:42.280
that on the day.

707
00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:43.200
I'm really proud of it.

708
00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:44.940
It completely sells it.

709
00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:49.400
Also, the way you stood back up, like you could tell things got a little dizzy for you.

710
00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:50.680
Yes, yes.

711
00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:55.880
This next storyline really sets up Andy and Erin for the whole day.

712
00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:59.980
Erin is going to ask Andy to help her with her scavenger hunt.

713
00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:01.400
It's a treasure hunt.

714
00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:04.040
You know, I think I'm more on Erin's side on this.

715
00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:05.560
I think it is a scavenger hunt.

716
00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:06.600
What's the difference?

717
00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:08.940
Oh, I know the difference.

718
00:33:09.180 --> 00:33:09.440
What?

719
00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:09.960
Okay.

720
00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:14.200
Scavenger hunt is when you have a piece of paper and you have all these different things

721
00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:15.280
you have to check off.

722
00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:18.540
So you've given those to your kids before, right?

723
00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:19.120
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

724
00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:19.520
Yeah.

725
00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:24.380
So that's a scavenger hunt because you're collecting multiple items or you're checking

726
00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:29.500
off multiple items, like find some sticks, find a rock, find a penny, find a leaf that's

727
00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:29.780
orange.

728
00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:32.560
And it all builds into a little hut at the end.

729
00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:34.080
Well, it builds into nothing.

730
00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:34.880
That's the point.

731
00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:41.720
A treasure hunt is one clue after another leading to an ultimate treasure, a single item.

732
00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:43.120
Oh, well, okay.

733
00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:47.380
So I guess whatever it is, it's a lot of work.

734
00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:48.100
It is.

735
00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:55.740
If you go to four minutes, 19 seconds, Andy is going to say, okay, fine, at least until

736
00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:56.920
Stanley calms down.

737
00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:58.340
And Erin's like, oh, is he?

738
00:33:58.460 --> 00:33:59.280
And Andy's like, don't look.

739
00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:01.240
I accidentally did his Sudoku.

740
00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:05.540
And then there's the shot of Stanley glaring at Andy.

741
00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:07.920
That was all scripted.

742
00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:11.320
Robert said he wrote this moment for his mom.

743
00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:15.980
He said his mom loves the character of Stanley and Sudoku.

744
00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:20.020
And he wanted to make sure that there was a funny Stanley moment in the script.

745
00:34:20.240 --> 00:34:24.520
He had been thinking about it from the day he was told he was going to work on The Office.

746
00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:27.500
That is so sweet.

747
00:34:27.500 --> 00:34:28.240
I know.

748
00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:30.040
Oh, my gosh.

749
00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:32.840
I love that that moment was for his mom.

750
00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:33.240
Yeah.

751
00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:36.720
Two of her favorite things, Stanley and Sudoku.

752
00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:40.020
